stablishes existence of some graph with these parameters if and only if $n\\in\\{3,4,5,8\\}$n∈{3,4,5,8} or $n\\geq 11$n≥11; the conjecture strengthens the large-order existence assertion by requiring the graph to be circulant, based on computational evidence.\n\n# \n\nCirculant graph conjecture for monophonic position number two\n\nLet $n$n be an integer, and let a circulant graph mean a graph whose vertices are arranged cyclically with adjacency determined by a fixed set of cyclic differences. The monophonic position number is denoted by $a$a, and the diameter by $D$D. Circulant graph conjecture. For any $n\\geq 11$n≥11, there is a circulant graph with order $n$n, monophonic position number $a=2$a=2, and diameter $D=2$D=2. The preceding theorem establishes existence of some graph with these parameters if and only if $n\\in\\{3,4,5,8\\}$n∈{3,4,5,8} or $n\\geq 11$n≥11; the conjecture strengthens the large-order existence assertion by requiring the graph to be circulant, based on computational evidence.\n\n## Progress summary\n\nRefresh\n\nNothing recorded yet. Refresh searches the literature and the public web for attempts on this problem, and writes the first summary here.\n\nSources & references View supporting material\n\n## Primary source\n\nJames Tuite, Elias John Thomas and Ullas Chandran S. V., “On some extremal position problems for graphs”, arXiv:2106.06827 (2022).\n\ncirculant-graphsgraph-theorymetric-graph-theory\n\n0\n\nPosted by arXiv 6 days ago\n\n## Research in progress\n\nDurable attempts, artifacts, checks, and next steps that another researcher can continue.\n\nNo shared research yet.\n\n## 0 Replies\n\n* * *\n\nSign in to reply.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nContents IJPAM: Volume 86, No. 4 (2013) (https://ijpam.eu/contents/2013-86-4/7/)\nciteturn1search2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 9.1 years ago; Key Words and Phrases: chromatic number, circulant graphs, coloring, prime distance graphs\n\n# IJPAM: Volume 86, No. 4 (2013)\n\nVERTEX COLORING OF CERTAIN DISTANCE GRAPHS\n\nV. Yegnanarayanan^{1}, A. Parthiban^{2}\n^{1,2}Department of Mathematics\nVelammal Engineering College\nAmbattur-Redhills Road, Chennai, 600 066, Tamil Nadu, INDIA\n\nAbstract. In this paper first, we give a brief introduction about integer distance graphs. An integer distance graph is a graph Image: $G(Z,D)$ with the set of integers as vertex set and an edge joining two vertices Image: $u$ and Image: $v$ if and only if Image: $\\vert u-v\\vert\\in D$ where Image: $D$ is a subset of the positive integers. If Image: $D$ is a subset of Image: $P$ then we call Image: $G(Z,D)$ a prime distance graph. Second, we obtain a partial solution to a general open problem of characterizing a class of prime distance graphs. Third, we compute the vertex arboricity of certain prime distance graphs. Fourth, we give a brief review regarding circulant graphs and highlight its importance in the computation of chromatic number of distance graphs with appropriate references. Fifth, we introduce the notion of pseudochromatic coloring and obtain certain results concerning circulant graphs and distance graphs.\n\nReceived: April 1, 2013\n\nAMS Subject Classification: 05C15\n\nKey Words and Phrases: chromatic number, circulant graphs, coloring, prime distance graphs\n\nDownload paper from here.\n\nDOI: 10.12732/ijpam.v86i4.7 How to cite this paper?\nSource: International Journal of Pure and Applied Mathematics\nISSN printed version: 1311-8080\nISSN on-line version: 1314-3395\nYear: 2013\nVolume: 86\nIssue: 4--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nFinite Prime Distance Graphs and 2-Odd Graphs (https://arxiv.org/abs/2106.02177)\nciteturn1academia15 [wordlim: 200] Published: 5.4 years ago; We prove that trees, cycles, and bipartite graphs are prime distance graphs, and that Dutch windmill graphs and paper mill graphs are prime distance graphs if and only if the Twin Prime Conjecture and dePolignac's Conjecture are true, respectively. We give a characterization of 2-odd graphs in terms of edge colorings, and we use this characterization to determine which circulant graphs of the form $Circ(n, \\{1,k\\})$ are 2-odd and to prove results on circulant prime distance graphs.\nTitle: Finite Prime Distance Graphs and 2-Odd Graphs\nAuthors: Joshua D. Laison, Colin Starr, Andrea Walker\nDate: Thu Jun  3 23:50:00 2021\n\nA graph $G$ is a prime distance graph (respectively, a 2-odd graph) if its vertices can be labeled with distinct integers such that for any two adjacent vertices, the difference of their labels is prime (either 2 or odd). We prove that trees, cycles, and bipartite graphs are prime distance graphs, and that Dutch windmill graphs and paper mill graphs are prime distance graphs if and only if the Twin Prime Conjecture and dePolignac's Conjecture are true, respectively. We give a characterization of 2-odd graphs in terms of edge colorings, and we use this characterization to determine which circulant graphs of the form $Circ(n, \\{1,k\\})$ are 2-odd and to prove results on circulant prime distance graphs.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nDistance magic circulant graphs - ScienceDirect (https://doi.org/10.1016/J.DISC.2015.07.002)\nciteturn1search3 [wordlim: 200] Published: 10.8 years ago; Crawled: 2 months ago; In this paper we deal with circulant graphs ${C}_{n}(1,p)$. ... Probably the best known problem in this area is the antimagic conjecture by Hartsfield and Ringel  [10], which claims that the edges of every graph except {K}_{2}${K}_{2}$ can be labeled by integers $1,2,\\dots ,\\left|E\\right|$ so that the weight of each vertex is different. ... The fundamental theorem of finite Abelian groups states that a finite Abelian group \\Gamma $\\Gamma $ can be expressed as a direct sum of cyclic subgroups of prime-power order. ... We have answered the question of existence of distance magic circulant graphs ${C}_{n}(1,p)$ completely for odd p$p$, and presented a construction of one class for p$p$ even.\n\n# Distance magic circulant graphs\n\nAuthor links open overlay panelSylwia Cichacz ^{a}, Dalibor Froncek ^{b}\n\nShow more\n\nView PDFDownload full issue\n\nCite\n\nAdd to Mendeley\n\nShare\n\n10.1016/j.disc.2015.07.002\n\nMore actions\n\n  * Article\n  * Recommended articles\n  * Cited by (33)\n\n## Abstract\n\nLet $G=(V,E)$ be a graph of order n$n$. A distance magic labeling of G$G$ is a bijection $\\ell :V\\to \\{1,2,\\dots ,n\\}$ for which there exists a positive integer k$k$ such that ${\\sum }_{x\\in N\\left(v\\right)}\\ell \\left(x\\right)=k$ for all $v\\in V$, where $N\\left(v\\right)$ is the neighborhood of v$v$. In this paper we deal with circulant graphs ${C}_{n}(1,p)$. The circulant graph ${C}_{n}(1,p)$ is the graph on the vertex set $V=\\{{x}_{0},{x}_{1},\\dots ,{x}_{n-1}\\}$ with edges ({x}_{i},{x}_{i+p})$({x}_{i},{x}_{i+p})$ for $i=0,\\dots ,n-1$ where $i+p$ is taken modulo n$n$. We completely characterize distance magic graphs ${C}_{n}(1,p)$ for p$p$ odd. We also give some sufficient conditions for p$p$ even. If the weight of each element is required to be equal, then we speak about magic-type labeling; when the weights are all different (or even form an arithmetic progression), then we speak about an antimagic-type labeling. Probably the best known problem in this area is the antimagic conjecture by Hartsfield and Ringel  [10], which claims that the edges of every graph except {K}_{2}${K}_{2}$ can be labeled by integers $1,2,\\dots ,\\left|E\\right|$ so that the weight of each vertex is different.\n\nA comprehensive dynamic survey of graph labelings is maintained by Gallian  [9]. A more detailed survey related to our topic by Arumugam et al.  [1] was published recently.\n\nA distance magic labeling (also called sigma labeling) of a graph $G=(V,E)$ of order n$n$ is a bijection $\\ell :V\\to \\{1,2,\\dots ,n\\}$ with the property that there is a positive integer k$k$ (called the magic constant) such that $w\\left(x\\right)=\\sum _{y\\in N\\left(x\\right)}\\ell \\left(y\\right)=k\\phantom{\\rule{1em}{0ex}}\\text{for every}\\phantom{\\rule{0ex}{0ex}}x\\in V\\left(G\\right)\\text{,}$ where $w\\left(x\\right)$ is the weight of vertex x$x$. If a graph G$G$ admits a distance magic labeling, then we say that G$G$ is a distance magic graph.\n\nIt is worth mentioning that finding an r$r$-regular distance magic labeling turns out to be equivalent to finding equalized incomplete tournament $\\mathrm{EIT}(n,r)$   [8]. In an equalized incomplete tournament $\\mathrm{EIT}(n,r)$ of n$n$ teams with r$r$ rounds, every team plays exactly r$r$ other teams and the total strength of the opponents that team i$i$ plays is k$k$. Thus, it is easy to notice that finding an $\\mathrm{EIT}(n,r)$ is the same as finding a distance magic labeling of any r$r$-regular graph on n$n$ vertices.\n\nThe following observations were proved independently:\n\nObservation 1\n\nMoreover every cyclic group of even order has exactly one involution. The fundamental theorem of finite Abelian groups states that a finite Abelian group \\Gamma $\\Gamma $ can be expressed as a direct sum of cyclic subgroups of prime-power order. This product is unique up to the order of the subgroups. When t$t$ is the number of these cyclic components whose order is a power of 2, then \\Gamma $\\Gamma $ has ${2}^{t}-1$ involutions. Moreover the sum of all the group elements is equal to the sum of the involutions. Let us denote this sum as $s\\left(\\Gamma \\right)={\\sum }_{g\\in \\Gamma }g$.\n\n## 4. Conclusion\n\nWe have answered the question of existence of distance magic circulant graphs ${C}_{n}(1,p)$ completely for odd p$p$, and presented a construction of one class for p$p$ even. Hence, we pose the following open problem.\n\nProblem 28\n\nCharacterize all distance magic circulant graphs ${C}_{n}(1,p)$ for p$p$ even.\n\nBased on our observations in Section  3